{#
    What the Membership Plan is, said by naming the things you do not have. `moments/Gate`, its second
    pane, the one the design system captions `GET /me/membership`, when you are not a member.

    Parameters:
      features  list of {name, where, href}: one gated feature, the page it affects, and optionally
                that page's URL. `where` is prose rather than a path, and the next paragraph is why
      heading   optional, defaults to `Membership Plan`
      sentence  optional, one line under the heading. The product's own voice, so it is set in
                `.voice` rather than in `.body`
      link      optional, {label, href}: the way to the membership page, for a caller that is not
                the membership page. The membership page itself overrides the `action` block below
                with its join forms instead, because it is already where the link would have gone

    AN UPSELL THAT DOES NOT SAY WHAT IS GATED IS A PAYWALL WITH A SLOGAN, which is `moments/Gate`'s
    own sentence and the reason the feature list is required rather than optional. A gated feature
    you cannot see is a gated feature nobody buys; a list of four named things with the page each
    one affects is what lets a reader go and look at what they would be buying.

    `where` IS PROSE AND NOT A URL, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE. The design
    system's preview writes `see who liked you &middot; /me/admirers`, and `bin/console debug:router`
    has no `/me/admirers`, no discovery feed and no pair page: matching/02, matching/04, matching/07
    and matching/10 are the four cards that gate these features and not one of them has landed. A
    URL printed in mono that answers `404` is worse than a phrase, because it reads as a promise the
    router does not keep. So each entry may carry an `href`, the phrase becomes a link the moment
    one is passed, and the membership page passes none today.

    THERE IS NO LOCK DRAWN HERE, and `moments/Gate` says why in one line: *"The lock is drawn once,
    at 20px, and it is the only icon in the product that means money. It never appears on something
    that is merely unavailable."* The lock belongs on the gated **control**, in the component's first
    pane, which is matching/04's rewind button and matching/07's add-a-day button. This partial is
    the other half of the same component, and a lock on a panel that is entirely about money would
    be the icon saying what every word around it already says.

    THIS IS A NARROWER REPRESENTATION AND NOT A REFUSAL. ADR 008's fourth question is entitlement,
    and its answer is *"`422` with what would lift it, or a narrower representation"*; ADR 009 rule 1
    says the representation is chosen by `Accept` and never by who is asking. Both hold here at once:
    `GET /subscriptions` is one URL and one provider, and a reader with no membership is served the
    same representation with nothing in it. Nothing is refused, so nothing answers `403`, and this
    panel is what the page draws instead of an empty list. ADR 008 writes the argument out at
    `docs/ADR/008_security.md`, "An entitlement is an upsell, not a wall".
#}
<div class="upsell">
    <h3 class="title" style="margin:0">{{ heading|default('Membership Plan') }}</h3>

    {% if sentence is defined and sentence is not empty %}
        <p class="voice" style="margin:var(--space-2) 0 0">{{ sentence }}</p>
    {% endif %}

    {#
        The four, by name, each with the page it affects. `.upsell__features` is the design system's
        own class and it carries the indent and the muted ink, so there is nothing to style here.
    #}
    <ul class="upsell__features body-sm">
        {% for feature in features %}
            <li>
                {{ feature.name }} &middot;
                {% if feature.href is defined and feature.href is not null %}
                    <a href="{{ feature.href }}">{{ feature.where }}</a>
                {% else %}
                    {{ feature.where }}
                {% endif %}
            </li>
        {% endfor %}
    </ul>

    {#
        The control, which the design system's preview draws as one `Join` button and which this
        partial leaves to the caller.

        It is a block rather than a parameter because a transition is a form, by ADR 009 rule 6, and
        a form is markup rather than a {label, href} pair: it carries `data-action`, `data-method`
        and its hidden fields, and no hash of scalars can express that without this file learning
        what a Membership Plan plan is. `components/_field.html.twig` makes the same move with its
        `control` block, for the same reason.

        The default arm is the link, for a caller that is drawing this panel somewhere a write would
        be out of place: matching/02's quota refusal in the middle of the deck, for instance, where
        the right control is a way to the membership page rather than a join form inside the feed.
    #}
    {% block action %}
        {% if link is defined and link is not null %}
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="{{ link.href }}">{{ link.label }}</a>
        {% endif %}
    {% endblock %}
</div>
